#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Virtual display for Sunshine on GNOME Wayland.

Injects an EDID into a free DRM connector (through a small root helper), then
lays out the desktop on it through Mutter's D-Bus API, so that Moonlight
streams a monitor that matches the client's screen.

Mutter is the source of truth. The scale, rotation and position chosen in
Settings > Displays are kept as they are, from one stream to the next. This
tool only touches the layout when it is unusable: the virtual monitor sits on
top of a real one, mirrors it, or is detached (the pointer could not cross).

Four modes, stored in ~/.local/state/gnome-virtual-display/mode:

  base    physical monitors only, virtual monitor off (connector still plugged)
  extend  physical monitors + virtual monitor on the right
  only    virtual monitor alone, physical monitors off
  remote  virtual monitor alone at `remote_refresh` Hz (streams over slow links)

Usage:
  gnome-virtual-display list          # outputs and layout
  gnome-virtual-display extend        # extended desktop
  gnome-virtual-display on            # alias of extend
  gnome-virtual-display only          # the virtual monitor alone
  gnome-virtual-display remote        # the virtual monitor alone at remote_refresh Hz
  gnome-virtual-display base          # base layout
  gnome-virtual-display wait MODE     # Sunshine detached command: applies MODE, waits for
                                      # the end of the stream, goes back to base
  gnome-virtual-display hold          # Sunshine app command: the app stays launched while
                                      # it runs; `wait` ends it when the stream drops
  gnome-virtual-display undo          # Sunshine undo: base, or the layout kept for a
                                      # reconnection after a dropped stream
  gnome-virtual-display sync-apps     # updates our Sunshine entries (format; Extend only
                                      # with a physical monitor plugged in)
  gnome-virtual-display rotate R [CONNECTOR]  # rotates a monitor (normal, left, right,
                                      # inverted); default: the first physical monitor
  gnome-virtual-display scale N [CONNECTOR]   # scale (1.0, 1.25, 1.3333, 1.6667, 2.0);
                                      # default: the virtual monitor
  gnome-virtual-display prep MODE     # Sunshine prep-cmd: claims the session, then applies MODE
  gnome-virtual-display boot          # at login: plugs the virtual connector (off), applies base
  gnome-virtual-display reapply       # reapplies the current mode (Sunshine start)
  gnome-virtual-display off           # removes the virtual monitor (unplugs the connector)
  gnome-virtual-display watch         # watches display changes (service)
  gnome-virtual-display fix-session   # frozen desktop: restarts GDM (login screen)
  gnome-virtual-display login-guard   # no session: restarts Sunshine if its start failed (service)

Configuration: ~/.config/gnome-virtual-display/config.toml, or the file named
by $GVD_CONFIG. Every key is optional except `connector`:

  connector       free DRM connector that receives the EDID, as listed in
                  /sys/class/drm without the cardN- prefix (required)
  edid            EDID injected by the helper, readable by the user
                  (/etc/gnome-virtual-display/edid.bin)
  fallback_scale  scale of a virtual monitor never configured (1.25)
  remote_refresh  refresh rate of the `remote` mode (60.0)
  grace           seconds a stream may resume after an abrupt end (90)
  sunshine_unit   Sunshine's systemd user unit (app-dev.lizardbyte.app.Sunshine.service)
  sunshine_log    Sunshine's log file (~/.config/sunshine/sunshine.log)
  sunshine_apps   Sunshine's apps file (~/.config/sunshine/apps.json)
  manage_sunshine_apps  keep our entries in that file current (true)
  flat_mouse_during_stream  flat mouse acceleration while streaming (true)
  helper          root helper, run as `sudo -n HELPER on|off CONNECTOR`
                  (/usr/local/sbin/gnome-virtual-display-drm)

Exit code 2: missing or invalid configuration. Exit code 3: gnome-shell does
not answer on D-Bus (cause in the journal, `journalctl -t gnome-virtual-display`).
Without a GNOME session (login screen), `prep`, `wait`, `base` and `reapply` do
nothing and exit 0: Moonlight streams the login screen, so the password can be
typed remotely.
"""
